+# Analysis Report — STORY-017: Target Async Reports Framework
+
+**Phase:** 4 — Analysis
+**Date:** 2026-04-29
+**Story scope:** Medium (`1 → 4 → 6 → 6b → 7 → 8 → 8b → 11 → Done`)
+**Blocked by:** STORY-007 (Target staging credentials — external, unresolved)
+
+---
+
+## Summary
+
+| Metric | Value |
+|--------|-------|
+| Approaches evaluated | 3 |
+| Top recommendation | Approach A — asyncio In-Process Poller + Postgres JSONB + YAML Registry |
+| Confidence | High |
+| Divergent assessments | 1 (Approach B: Technical vs Business) |
+
+---
+
+## Context Weights
+
+| Dimension | Weight | Rationale |
+|-----------|--------|-----------|
+| Technical Soundness | 20% | Greenfield, not yet production-critical; correctness over elegance |
+| Future Flexibility | 25% | Foundation/platform story — "new report types = config change" is the explicit goal; extensibility is the primary value |
+| Business Value | 20% | Internal ops tool; all ACs are clearly defined; completeness is achievable with any approach |
+| Implementation Effort | 20% | Blocked on STORY-007, no hard deadline; effort matters but is not the dominant constraint |
+| Risk Profile | 15% | API write safety policy is non-negotiable, but report requests are low blast-radius vs. pricing/inventory writes |
+
+---
+
+## Approaches Evaluated
+
+| # | Name | Lifecycle | Storage | Registry |
+|---|------|-----------|---------|----------|
+| A | asyncio In-Process Poller + Postgres JSONB + YAML Registry | asyncio task / APScheduler, state in Postgres | JSONB in `report_documents` table | `registry.yaml` loaded at startup, Pydantic-validated |
+| B | Background Worker (Celery/ARQ) + Blob Storage + DB Registry | Celery/ARQ task queue, Redis broker, state in Postgres | S3/MinIO blob + Postgres metadata | Postgres `report_types` table, CRUD API |
+| C | FastAPI BackgroundTask + Filesystem + YAML Registry | `FastAPI.BackgroundTasks`, state in Postgres | Local filesystem + Postgres metadata | `registry.yaml` (identical to A) |
+
+---
+
+## Evaluation Agents
+
+| Agent | Dimensions | Approaches Scored |
+|-------|-----------|------------------|
+| Technical | Technical Soundness + Future Flexibility | 3 |
+| Business | Business Value + Implementation Effort | 3 |
+| Risk | Risk Profile + Risk Register | 3 |
+
+---
+
+## Scoring Matrix
+
+| Approach | Technical (20%) | Flexibility (25%) | Business Value (20%) | Impl Effort (20%) | Risk (15%) | **Weighted Total** |
+|----------|:--------------:|:----------------:|:-------------------:|:-----------------:|:----------:|:------------------:|
+| A: asyncio + Postgres JSONB + YAML | 3/5 | 3/5 | 4/5 | 5/5 | 3/5 | **3.60** |
+| B: Celery/ARQ + Blob + DB Registry | 4/5 | 4/5 | 3/5 | 2/5 | 2/5 | **3.10** |
+| C: BackgroundTask + Filesystem + YAML | 2/5 | 2/5 | 3/5 | 4/5 | 2/5 | **2.60** |
+
+_Weighted total = (Tech × 0.20) + (Flex × 0.25) + (BV × 0.20) + (Effort × 0.20) + (Risk × 0.15)_
+
+---
+
+## Score Justifications
+
+### Approach A: asyncio In-Process Poller + Postgres JSONB + YAML Registry
+
+**Technical Soundness: 3/5**
+- Architecture: asyncio poll loop inside FastAPI (or APScheduler) is an adequate fit for 10–50 requests/day; conflates HTTP server lifecycle with background job lifecycle
+- Security: Postgres JSONB is auditable, transactional, no blob credentials to manage; low credential surface
+- Reliability: Process restart drops in-flight polling state; startup recovery sweep required (not built by default); a missed sweep leaves `PENDING` rows orphaned indefinitely
+- Maintainability: Entire lifecycle in one codebase, no separate worker to deploy or debug; YAML + Pydantic is clean
+- Performance: Handles 10–50 req/day with massive headroom; begins degrading above ~500 concurrent in-flight polls (not a foreseeable constraint here)
+
+**Future Flexibility: 3/5**
+- Lock-in: APScheduler not portable to proper task queue later (lifecycle rewrite required); YAML registry and Postgres state machine survive any migration
+- Extensibility: New report type = one YAML line (zero code change) — excellent SC-3 alignment; new status values require schema migration but no lifecycle rewrite
+- Scale ceiling: Handles ~500/day before asyncio thread-pool saturation; rework required at ~1,000+/day sustained (not realistic for single-seller Target Plus in the near term)
+- Upgrade path: Postgres state machine + YAML registry are forward-compatible; APScheduler → ARQ/Celery is a lifecycle-layer rewrite, not a full redesign
+
+**Business Value: 4/5**
+- Problem fit: Covers all 5 ACs cleanly; SC-3 (config-driven registry) is the strongest implementation of the stated goal
+- User impact: Simple mental model for operators: submit → poll → done; queryable status via Postgres
+- Time-to-value: Fastest path to SC-1 passing in staging — no infra provisioning required
+- Completeness: All ACs addressable; startup sweep gap is recoverable, not a blocker
+
+**Implementation Effort: 5/5**
+- Development time: ~3–4 days; asyncio + Postgres JSONB is standard FastAPI fare
+- Team fit: Excellent — Python + FastAPI + Postgres is the documented team stack; nothing new introduced
+- Learning curve: Near-zero; APScheduler and asyncio backoff are one-file concerns
+- Operational overhead: One process, one database; standard FastAPI deploy patterns unchanged
+
+**Risk Profile: 3/5** (Moderate)
+- Key risks: R-A-2 (restart drops polling state), R-A-3 (`tgtrt` log leak), R-A-4 (unknown enum crashes poller), R-A-10 (unknown report payload size)
+- All risks have clear mitigations; mitigations require disciplined implementation on greenfield, not just intention
+- See Risk Register below
+
+---
+
+### Approach B: Background Worker (Celery/ARQ) + Blob Storage + DB Registry
+
+**Technical Soundness: 4/5**
+- Architecture: Proper separation of concerns; HTTP layer enqueues, worker polls; ARQ (asyncio-native) preferred over Celery; proven pattern at scale
+- Security: S3/MinIO requires bucket policies, IAM keys, presigned URL TTL management — larger credential surface than Postgres JSONB; R-B-3 (`tgtrt` in broker payload) is a novel risk
+- Reliability: Strongest durability posture — ARQ requeues unacknowledged tasks on reconnect; Redis AOF prevents task loss; dead-letter queue for inspection
+- Maintainability: Adds Redis + MinIO + worker process to deploy, monitor, debug; log correlation across API and worker requires structured logging discipline
+- Performance: Handles 50,000+ requests/day; massively over-provisioned for 10–50/day
+
+**Future Flexibility: 4/5**
+- Lock-in: Redis + ARQ well-understood; S3/MinIO portable (env-var change via boto3); DB registry is the most extensible option
+- Extensibility: New report types via CRUD API (no restart); new output formats natural in blob storage; new status values require migration but no worker rewrite
+- Scale ceiling: No practical ceiling for this use case
+- Upgrade path: Postgres state machine + DB registry are forward-compatible with any future queue technology
+
+**Business Value: 3/5**
+- Problem fit: Covers all ACs; SC-3 requires nuance — DB CRUD API is "config change" in spirit but requires API call, not file edit
+- Time-to-value: Delayed — Redis + MinIO + worker must be provisioned and validated before SC-1 is testable
+- Strategic alignment: Best long-term for volume/concurrency; premature optimization at current scale
+
+**Implementation Effort: 2/5**
+- Development time: ~8–12 days; independent scope items: Celery/ARQ setup, broker provisioning, MinIO + S3 config, worker deploy, CRUD registry API
+- Team fit: Poor — no documented Celery/ARQ or S3/MinIO production experience; three new operational domains simultaneously
+- Learning curve: Significant; Celery task serialization, broker reconnect behavior, DLQ routing all non-trivial
+- Operational overhead: High — three new infra components (broker, worker, blob store), each requiring health checks, alerting, restart policies
+
+**Risk Profile: 2/5** (High)
+- Key risks: R-B-2 (team has no ARQ/Celery ops knowledge), R-B-3 (`tgtrt` in broker payload), R-B-4 (infra disproportionate to volume), R-B-7 (DB registry CRUD unauthenticated write surface)
+- R-B-2 + R-B-4 compound: three novel infra domains on a greenfield project; incident TTR will be disproportionately high
+- See Risk Register below
+
+---
+
+### Approach C: FastAPI BackgroundTask + Filesystem + YAML Registry
+
+**Technical Soundness: 2/5**
+- Architecture: `FastAPI.BackgroundTasks` designed for fire-and-forget (e.g., send email); using it for stateful polling with retry semantics exceeds its design envelope
+- Security: Filesystem has no access controls beyond OS permissions; container restart without volume mount destroys all downloaded reports silently
+- Reliability: Two compounding structural deficiencies — (1) uncaught exceptions swallowed silently with no retry, (2) filesystem non-durable without explicit volume mount; both occur in normal operations, not only under adversarial conditions
+- Maintainability: Simple codebase; but filesystem path management couples code to deployment environment, complicating testing and cross-env debugging
+- Performance: At 10–50/day, CPU non-issue; real failure mode is reliability, not throughput
+
+**Future Flexibility: 2/5**
+- Lock-in: `BackgroundTasks` is FastAPI-specific with no analog in other frameworks; migrating requires lifecycle rewrite + storage migration away from filesystem
+- Extensibility: YAML registry is excellent for SC-3 (same as A); adding output formats requires new file-write code; structured retry policies require bolt-on logic not natively supported
+- Scale ceiling: Above ~20–30 concurrent in-flight polls, BackgroundTasks shares Starlette thread pool with request handlers — latent request starvation risk
+- Upgrade path: Filesystem → Postgres or blob migration is a full storage migration; no portable upgrade path
+
+**Business Value: 3/5**
+- Problem fit: Covers all ACs in the happy path; SC-1 has reliability gap (restart drops polls); SC-3 strong via YAML
+- User impact: No SQL-queryable report content for operators (vs. Postgres JSONB in A); filesystem storage misaligned with "Amazon data warehouse" long-term goal
+- Completeness: Reliability gap is structural, not incidental — requires partially re-implementing a task queue to address
+
+**Implementation Effort: 4/5**
+- Development time: ~3–4 days (same as A); BackgroundTasks is zero-config
+- Team fit: Good — BackgroundTasks is standard FastAPI
+- Operational overhead: Low for task scheduling; non-trivial for filesystem: volume mount must be configured, permissions validated, backed up in staging and production
+
+**Risk Profile: 2/5** (High)
+- Key risks: R-C-1 (exceptions swallowed silently), R-C-3 (filesystem not durable), R-C-11 (R-C-1 + R-C-3 + restart compound = routine data loss)
+- R-C-1 + R-C-3 compound is the dominant concern: unlike Approach A where restart durability requires explicit implementation, Approach C cannot be made safe without replacing the core mechanism
+- See Risk Register below
+
+---
+
+## Divergent Assessments
+
+**Approach B — Technical (4/4) vs Business (3/2):**
+The Technical Evaluator assessed Approach B as the architecturally strongest approach, with a proper separation of concerns, durable task queue semantics, and no practical scale ceiling. The Business Evaluator assessed it as low-value for this story because the team has no ARQ/Redis/S3 operational experience, and the infra footprint is disproportionate to a 10–50 req/day internal ops tool. **Both perspectives have merit.** Approach B is the right architecture at 10× volume with a team that has broker+blob experience — it is the wrong approach today on a greenfield project with no operational patterns established. This is a "right answer, wrong context" divergence. The Business score dominates in the weighted total because effort and risk carry 35% combined weight and both assess the team-fit dimension harshly.
+
+---
+
+## Top 3 Ranking
+
+### 1. Approach A — asyncio In-Process Poller + Postgres JSONB + YAML Registry (Weighted: 3.60)
+
+**Why #1:** Dominates on the two highest-weighted dimensions (Future Flexibility 25% via YAML registry, Implementation Effort 20% with 5/5 team fit) and has the best risk profile of the three. All failure modes have clear mitigations that Phase 6 can mandate in the spec. The architecture is appropriate for the problem scale.
+
+**Key strength:** YAML registry is the canonical expression of SC-3 ("new report types = config change, not code change") — it requires zero code change and zero API call to add a new type.
+
+**Key risk:** R-A-2 — process restart drops in-flight polling state. The startup sweep for stale `PENDING` rows is the critical mitigation and must be explicitly specified in `feature-spec.md` or it will be cut under delivery pressure.
+
+**Trade-off:** Gives up the durable task-queue semantics of Approach B. Acceptable at 10–50 req/day with a disciplined startup sweep; revisit if volume exceeds ~500/day sustained.
+
+---
+
+### 2. Approach B — Background Worker (Celery/ARQ) + Blob Storage + DB Registry (Weighted: 3.10)
+
+**Why #2:** Strongest architecture technically and highest future flexibility ceiling. Would rank #1 on a team with existing ARQ + S3 operational experience. Loses to A because the combined team-fit penalty on effort (2/5) and operational risk (2/5) is too large for a 10–50 req/day greenfield tool.
+
+**Key strength:** Only approach with true durable task-queue semantics — worker restart, broker reconnect, and DLQ are handled by the platform, not by custom code.
+
+**Key risk:** R-B-2 + R-B-4 — team acquires three novel operational domains (ARQ, Redis, S3/MinIO) simultaneously. First production incident will be slow to resolve; no runbooks, no prior patterns.
+
+**Trade-off:** Defer until the team has operated simpler infrastructure for a release cycle, or if volume projections exceed 1,000+ req/day.
+
+---
+
+### 3. Approach C — FastAPI BackgroundTask + Filesystem + YAML Registry (Weighted: 2.60)
+
+**Why #3:** Implementation effort is nearly as good as Approach A (4/5 vs 5/5), but two structural reliability failures (R-C-1 + R-C-3) that compound during normal operations make it a materially worse foundation than Approach A. At approximately equal implementation cost, Approach A is strictly better.
+
+**Key strength:** YAML registry is identical to Approach A; zero new infra beyond Approach A.
+
+**Key risk:** R-C-1 + R-C-3 compounding — exceptions are silently dropped AND filesystem is non-durable across container restarts. These are not risks requiring adversarial conditions; they occur on every deploy and every container replacement.
+
+**Trade-off:** Not recommended. Approach A delivers the same simplicity benefits with better reliability characteristics at negligible additional implementation cost.
+
