+
+## Risk Register Highlights (Top 3 approaches)
+
+| ID | Approach | Risk | Likelihood | Severity | Mitigation | Residual |
+|----|----------|------|-----------|----------|-----------|----------|
+| R-A-2 | A | Process restart drops all in-flight poll tasks; `PENDING` rows orphaned | High | High | Startup sweep: query `PENDING` rows older than N seconds, re-enqueue; `last_polled_at` + `attempts` columns | Medium |
+| R-A-3 | A | `tgtrt` credential leaks into logs via httpx debug logging or exception tracebacks | Medium | Critical | httpx event hook strips auth headers before any log write; Phase 7 `test_tgtrt_redacted.py` assertion | Low |
+| R-A-4 | A | Unknown status enum values (OQ-11) throw `ValueError`, crashing the poller | Medium | High | `status_raw` passthrough: unknown values stored as string, tagged `UNRECOGNIZED_STATUS`, lifecycle continues | Low |
+| R-A-6 | A | PII in report payload persisted to Postgres without review | Unknown | Critical | Phase 6b hard gate before `storage.py` design; `pii_reviewed` flag on table | Low (if gate enforced) |
+| R-A-10 | A | Large report payloads degrade Postgres JSONB performance | Unknown | Medium | `max_document_bytes` guard with `DOCUMENT_TOO_LARGE` status; STORY-007 must measure payload size | Medium |
+| R-B-2 | B | Team has no ARQ/Redis/S3 operational knowledge; incident TTR will be very high | High | High | Prefer ARQ over Celery; write runbooks in Phase 10 before production | Medium |
+| R-B-3 | B | `tgtrt` serialized into broker task payload, persisted in Redis | High | Critical | Never pass `tgtrt` as task arg; re-fetch from secrets store inside task body | Low (if enforced) |
+| R-C-1 | C | Uncaught exception in `BackgroundTask` silently swallowed; no retry; `PENDING` stuck | High | High | `try/except` entire body; update DB to `FAILED`; startup sweep — but this re-implements a task queue | Medium |
+| R-C-3 | C | Filesystem non-durable across container restarts without explicit volume mount | High | High | Named Docker volume; Phase 11 pre-deploy gate verifies mount | Medium |
+
+---
+
+## Recommendation
+
+**Select Approach A** (asyncio In-Process Poller + Postgres JSONB + YAML Registry).
+
+The framework's core value proposition — "new report types require a config change, not a code change" — is best expressed by the YAML registry. Postgres JSONB storage keeps report data queryable alongside other operational data (consistent with the long-term "same visibility as Amazon" goal). The asyncio poller is appropriate for 10–50 req/day with substantial headroom.
+
+What is sacrificed: durable task-queue semantics (Approach B) and zero infrastructure overhead (all approaches). The deficit is manageable: the startup recovery sweep, `status_raw` passthrough, and `tgtrt` event-hook redaction must be explicitly specified in `feature-spec.md` — Phase 6 cannot treat them as implementation details.
+
+**Phase 6 must mandate the following before Phase 8 can begin:**
+
+1. Startup sweep for stale `PENDING` rows (R-A-2) — specified in `feature-spec.md`, not left to implementer
+2. `status_raw` passthrough + `UNRECOGNIZED_STATUS` terminal state (R-A-4) — in state machine design
+3. `tgtrt` redaction via httpx event hook (R-A-3) — in `TargetReportsClient` spec
+4. `max_document_bytes` guard with `DOCUMENT_TOO_LARGE` status (R-A-10) — sized against STORY-007 output
+5. PII classification for `ALLOWED_ITEM_TYPES` payload (R-A-6) — Phase 6b hard gate before `storage.py` schema is finalized
+
+**Revisit Approach B** if: (a) volume projections exceed 500+ req/day sustained, or (b) the team acquires ARQ+Redis operational experience from another project.
